.Dd September 16, 2026
.Dt NDI_DEVI_ONLINE 9F
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm ndi_devi_bind_driver ,
.Nm ndi_devi_bind_driver_async ,
.Nm ndi_devi_online ,
.Nm ndi_devi_online_async ,
.Nm ndi_devi_offline
.Nd bind, attach, and offline device nodes
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/sunndi.h
.Ft "int"
.Fo ndi_devi_bind_driver
.Fa "dev_info_t *dip"
.Fa "uint_t flags"
.Fc
.Ft "int"
.Fo ndi_devi_bind_driver_async
.Fa "dev_info_t *dip"
.Fa "uint_t flags"
.Fc
.Ft "int"
.Fo ndi_devi_online
.Fa "dev_info_t *dip"
.Fa "uint_t flags"
.Fc
.Ft "int"
.Fo ndi_devi_online_async
.Fa "dev_info_t *dip"
.Fa "uint_t flags"
.Fc
.Ft "int"
.Fo ndi_devi_offline
.Fa "dev_info_t *dip"
.Fa "uint_t flags"
.Fc
.Sh INTERFACE LEVEL
illumos specific
.Pq Consolidation Private .
.Sh PARAMETERS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Va dip
Child device node to change.
.It Va flags
Operation flags, as described below.
.El
.Sh DESCRIPTION
\fBndi_devi_bind_driver()\fP links and binds \fBdip\fP to a driver without attaching
the device instance.
Set the node name and hardware properties, including any \fBcompatible\fP
property, before binding.
Pass zero for \fBflags\fP for ordinary binding.
.Pp
\fBndi_devi_online()\fP binds the node, merges applicable configuration-file
properties, clears the offline state, and attempts to attach the device.
It also attempts to configure descendants if the node is a nexus.
The return status describes the node's attachment; descendant configuration
can fail independently.
An attachment failure can free a nonpersistent node.
Do not assume such a pointer remains valid on failure.
.Pp
\fBndi_devi_offline()\fP detaches the device and its descendants and marks the
device offline to prevent deferred attachment.
Open devices or other references can prevent detachment.
With \fBNDI_DEVI_REMOVE\fP, it also removes the node from the tree.
The pointer is invalid after successful removal.
Other useful flags are \fBNDI_NO_EVENT\fP, to suppress device add/remove events,
and \fBNDI_DEVI_DEBUG\fP, to enable diagnostic messages.
Configuration flags are described in \fBndi_devi_config(9F)\fP.
.Pp
The asynchronous functions dispatch the corresponding operation to the
parent's device task queue.
The parent must have a device task queue and \fBdip\fP must be persistent.
The caller must ensure the node and parent remain valid until the queued work
has finished; dispatch does not acquire a device-node reference.
Use \fBNDI_NOSLEEP\fP when dispatching from interrupt context.
Without it, allocation and dispatch may sleep.
There is no completion callback or returned status for the deferred operation.
.Pp
The synchronous functions manage the relevant device-tree critical sections.
An offline operation may also clean cached devfs nodes.
\fBNDI_DEVFS_CLEAN\fP explicitly requests cleaning even when the caller already
owns the parent's critical section.
Do not use that flag from a nexus \fBbus_config\fP implementation, because devfs
lookup locks may already be held.
.Sh CONTEXT
The synchronous functions may be called from user or kernel context and
may sleep.
The asynchronous functions may also be called from low-level interrupt context
when \fBNDI_NOSLEEP\fP is specified.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
The synchronous functions return \fBNDI_SUCCESS\fP on success and an NDI
failure status otherwise.
Callers must compare explicitly against \fBNDI_SUCCESS\fP.
The asynchronous functions return \fBNDI_SUCCESS\fP when work has been queued,
or \fBNDI_FAILURE\fP if it could not be queued.
Successful dispatch does not imply successful binding or attachment.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr attach 9E ,
.Xr detach 9E ,
.Xr devfs_clean 9F ,
.Xr ndi_devi_alloc 9F ,
.Xr ndi_devi_config 9F ,
.Xr ndi_devi_enter 9F ,
.Xr sunndi 9F
